const PART: Record<string, string[]> = {
  "md:0": [
    "In Moldova, among vineyards and hills, there was a morning when the sun did not rise. The sky became like a wine-cellar roof, and the grapes did not know which way to ripen. In the village, a grandmother called Ioana lit the fire. She said to her grandson: 'This is not a cloud. Someone has taken the sun.' The boy was called Făt-Frumos, a name people give to a brave young man. He was no palace prince. He was the one who gets up when the vegetable garden goes dark.",
    "Ioana reminded him of the mill horse, thin from carrying sacks but with a lively eye. Făt-Frumos took bread and apples. The horse ate slowly, like someone accepting a contract. Then it spoke, like a horse that had crossed the Codru, the great forest. 'The zmei, the dragons of the stories here, have hidden the sun in a cave. I know the path. You take a flute, not a sword.' The boy promised. He mounted without pulling the rein hard. The horse found the dark way by itself.",
    "Deep in the cave, three zmei slept curled up, round as barrels. The sun was caught between their tails like a lamp. They were not monsters with bared teeth. They were stubborn guardians, convinced the light belonged to them. Făt-Frumos did not wake them with shouts. He sat on a stone and played his grandmother's flute. It was a harvest tune from the hills, slow and warm. The zmei snored deeper. The horse put its nose to the light, and the sun came loose like ripe fruit.",
    "Taking the sun outside needed both hands and no hurry. Every time the flute stopped, a zmei moved an eyelid. Făt-Frumos played again. The horse walked sideways to avoid the scales. At the mouth of the cave, the first ray hit the Codru's leaves. The trees straightened their shadows. The zmei stayed in their stone house, sleeping a nap they had asked for without knowing. Nobody needed to hurt anyone. The light left because it was asked for with music.",
    "The sun settled again in the sky over Moldova. The vines opened their faces. Chișinău, far off, had golden roofs. The village put bread on the table and water for the horse. Ioana said the sentence the tale keeps: you do not conquer the day with blows. Făt-Frumos hung the flute in the wine press and went to water the garden. The zmei are still in the cave, and now and then a morning is late. Then someone remembers to bring music instead of fear."
  ],
  "md:1": [
    "Beside a mill, on the road to Orhei, a boy found a very thin horse. Its ribs looked like a window frame. Other people walked past and said it was no use now. The boy was called Făt-Frumos by the village, because he was the one who does not walk on. He broke his bread in two. The horse ate half, drank at the ditch and looked at him closely. 'If you care for me for a week, I will show you what I know,' it said. It was not a market horse. It was a cal năzdrăvan, a horse that understands the world.",
    "For seven mornings the boy brought oats and brushed the coat. He did not ask the horse to work. The coat became silver. The legs that had trembled became wind. On the seventh morning the horse knelt for him to mount. Then it warned him: 'There is a pale tower on the cliffs. Ileana is there, and she waits for someone who arrives without spurs.' Ileana Cosânzeana is the young woman with an embroidered blouse and a flower crown, as clever as she is beautiful. She was not held by a bolt. She waited for a traveller who knew how to treat the weaker ones.",
    "The horse ran over the hills without treading on the vines. Below, the river Răut went past, and the cliffs of Orheiul Vechi opened like a book of stone. In the tower, which was more river glass than wall, Ileana was watering basil. She saw the silver horse and came down the stairs of her own free will. 'You do not come for a prize,' she said. 'You come because the mill taught you to stop.' Făt-Frumos took off his straw hat and agreed. There was no fight. There was an invitation to come down together.",
    "Ileana brought seeds from the tower and a sweet walnut bread, which in Moldova is broken with the hands. The horse carried the two of them without complaining, and even stopped to drink where the sheep drink. On the way they met a hedgehog and walked round it instead of pushing it. Ileana laughed: 'That is why the tower opened.' The boy understood that the năzdrăvan horse was not a shortcut to fame. It was a test lasting a whole week, made of oats, a brush and half a loaf.",
    "In the village, Ioana put the flower crown on Ileana's head. She put a blanket on the horse's back. Nobody asked about treasures. They asked whether the tower was cold and whether the river came high. The Moldovan tale ends at a table, not on a throne. The thin mill horse turns silver for whoever looks after it. Ileana chooses the house of someone who can wait seven mornings. If you meet someone tired at the edge of the road, break the bread. Sometimes that is how a whole story agrees to carry you."
  ],
  "md:2": [
    "Baba Dochia is the old woman of March. In Moldova people still say her name when the weather changes its mind. She lived high on a hill, with nine cloaks one over another. Each cloak was a different colour of wool. Her granddaughter brought her milk and a twisted thread of red and white. The thread is the mărțișor, a sign that spring must not forget winter. Dochia liked the cold, but she liked being right more. When she saw a lamb in the grass, she decided winter was over.",
    "She took off the first cloak and left it on a stone. The sun seemed to agree. The next day she took off the second, then the third, one for each morning. The shepherds warned her: 'Baba Dochia, March has not signed the peace yet.' She laughed with her mouth full of wind and showed them the lamb. The granddaughter tightened the mărțișor on her wrist and said no more. Sometimes old people also need to fool themselves a little, so the lesson has somewhere to sit.",
    "By the ninth morning she had no more cloaks. Her dress was light, and the vines below looked ready. Then March, who in the tale is a stubborn neighbour, sent back a night of snow. The flakes settled on Dochia's shoulders without a sound. She did not run down the hill shouting. She sat on the stone where she had left the first cloak. She pulled her granddaughter onto her lap while the girl was still near. 'Take the red and white thread,' she said. 'I will stay and watch the weather.'",
    "The snow covered her carefully. The hill had a new stone, round, with a shawl of lichen. It was not a fright. It was the tale's way of explaining the rocks that look at the vines. The granddaughter went down with the mărțișor. She told the village what her grandmother had asked. Wear the thread when March begins, red for the sun and white for the snow. It is a reminder that the two go together. The nine cloaks, they say, can still be seen in the stone's bands at nightfall.",
    "That is why, on the first day of March, people in Moldova give the thread. They do not mock anyone still wearing a coat. Baba Dochia teaches the opposite of hurry. Spring arrives when it arrives, and one lamb will not convince a hill. If you see snow on top of a flower, do not laugh at either of them. Give the thread to someone you like and leave the stone in peace. The old woman of March is still up there, counting the mornings."
  ],
  "md:3": [
    "Beside the cliffs of Orhei, the village had a small lake that fed the gardens and the barrels. One summer, the lake woke up empty. The fish were left in the mud, and the vines lowered their leaves. A girl called Ileana, in an embroidered ie, went down with a jug. An ie is a traditional blouse with wide sleeves. At the bottom she found a balaur, the great serpent of Moldovan tales. It did not breathe fire. It was coiled like a root, with scales the colour of clay and moss. It had drunk the water because nobody had spoken to it since winter.",
    "The village wanted sticks and shouting. Ileana asked for one day. She went up to the house and filled the jug with sweet grape must. Must is not party wine but harvest juice. She went back singing the song her mother used to put the well water to sleep. The balaur opened one golden eye, surprised not to be cursed at. 'I drank the lake because I was alone,' it said. 'The thirst was the size of me.' Its voice was like wet stone. Ileana set the jug within reach, without stepping inside the circle of scales.",
    "The balaur drank the must and became less dry inside. Ileana did not ask for the lake back like someone demanding a debt. She asked where the water had gone. 'It is with me,' it said. 'If you give me a neighbour's name, and not a frightened one, I will give it back in a spring.' She thought of the cliffs, of the monastery dug into the rock, of the sheep. 'You will be the guardian of the spring,' she said. 'Nobody throws stones at you, and you do not drink the garden.' The balaur agreed, because nobody had offered it a place for a long time.",
    "The water came out of its mouth like a pale ribbon, not like an attack. It ran to the lake until the fish had a roof again. The gardens lifted their leaves that same afternoon. The balaur climbed onto a ledge of the cliff. On hot days a dark patch can still be seen there, which the children say is its back. Ileana left the empty jug on the bank, turned upwards, so the rain would have somewhere to fall. The village came down without sticks.",
    "The tale stays with this combination, which Moldova likes to repeat. Great thirst is not beaten with blows, and a lake comes back when someone sits down to talk. The balaur did not become a lap dog. It stayed wild, in its own place, guarding the spring it had promised. Ileana went back to her embroidery with one more green thread on her sleeve, to remember the scale. If something in your home disappears, first ask who is thirsty. Sometimes the answer drinks from a jug and gives back the river."
  ],
  "md:4": [
    "On the two banks of the Nistru lived two sisters who could not fit in the same sky. The elder wanted to light the world in the morning. She wanted to keep the cockerels, the bread and the vines opening. The younger wanted the afternoon, the lamps and the river turning to silver. They argued without hurting each other, but they argued every day. The stars, tired, began to blink at odd hours. The Moon, round and patient, came down to the water to listen. It did not come to scold. It came to share out the work.",
    "'You,' it said to the elder, 'are the luceafăr de ziuă, the morning star. You light the path of the shepherds and vanish when the sun knows the names of people.' 'And you,' it said to the younger, 'are the luceafăr de noapte, the evening star. You light the windows and stay until the first sister is ready again.' Both thought it was too little. They both wanted the whole day. The Moon shook its face and showed them the river. Neither bank keeps all the water. The Nistru runs because nobody keeps it for themselves alone.",
    "The morning sister tried her turn. She rose blue and golden, set a cockerel crowing on every farm and warmed the stone of the well. When the sun came, she did not argue. She went down behind the hills, as agreed. She left a thin trail so her sister would know where to begin. On the other side of the river, the evening sister saw that trail. She understood it was not a defeat. It was a message. She lit the first lamp of a house in Chișinău without putting out the memory of morning.",
    "In the evening, the evening star went the way back. It turned the Nistru silver, counted the lit windows and waited. When the village slept, it did not keep the sky for ever. It leaned over, left the Moon in the middle, and made room for the glow from the east. The two crossed for an instant above the water and waved. Whoever was on the bridge saw two lights, not a quarrel. The boatmen like to say that is the best moment to cross. Neither sister pushes the other then.",
    "Since then, Moldova has the morning star and the evening star, and neither of them is less important. The tale is for siblings who want the same window. The sky is wide, the river has two banks, and taking turns is also a form of love. If you want the whole day just for yourself, look at the Nistru. The water does not choose a bank. And when you see one light rising as another sets, remember the sisters waving. Each is on her own side, without putting out the other."
  ],
  "me:0": [
    "Lake Skadar is so wide that the other shore looks like a cloud resting on the water. Lara lived in a stone house among reeds and water lilies. Her father caught only what the table asked for. One day the market in Podgorica paid well, and Lara filled the boat to the edge. The silver fish lay on top of each other. The pelicans, which used to land on the bow, stayed on the island. They did not follow her.",
    "The next morning the lake was too smooth. No fish jumped. The net came back empty, and the little island bells seemed further away. Lara sat on the jetty with the basket still smelling of scales. She understood that she had taken everyone's dinner, not just her own. Water, in Montenegro, is not a warehouse. It is a long table. She pushed the boat out again with the basket. She meant to return the fish still alive to a shaded tank.",
    "In the middle of the water lilies a vila rose up, a lake fairy. Her dress was the colour of the water, and a leaf pinned her hair. She did not come angry. She came tall and calm, like someone who has seen many boats. 'You fished as if the lake ended on Saturday,' she said. 'Give back what does not fit in the pot, and I will show you where the water still breathes.' Lara tilted her head, without fear, and opened the tank. The fish went back to the green, one by one. The vila smiled for the first time.",
    "The vila walked on the leaves without breaking them. She opened a narrow path between the islands. Where she passed, a small fish jumped like a silver comma. Lara did not throw the net. She only looked, to learn the places. She marked a reed with a red ribbon so her father could come back. The mark showed him how to fish without emptying the bottom. Far off, Podgorica was a dust of roofs. Near, the lake made again the small sound of a place that is lived in.",
    "At home, the pot had enough fish and bread was left over for the neighbour. Her father did not scold. He asked where the ribbon was, and Lara told him about the vila. She did not make the fairy bigger than she was. In Montenegro they say Lake Skadar is still wide because someone, once, gave back what was too much. If you go to a place that feeds many people, take only your share. The fairy does not ask for a speech. She asks that the boat comes back lighter than it went."
  ],
  "me:1": [
    "In the Durmitor, the high mountain of Montenegro, summer dried up the village stream. The sheep licked stone, and the well returned the bucket with a hollow sound. Nikola was a boy who hunted only for his grandmother's pot. That summer he climbed higher than usual, among black pines. In a clearing he saw a deer with antlers shining like two small moons. His hand went to the bow out of habit. Then he stopped. A light like that is not for eating.",
    "The deer did not run. It looked at Nikola as if judging whether he would lower the bow. Nikola lowered it, took out the arrow and leaned the weapon against a trunk. 'If you are from here, show me water,' he said. He felt a little silly talking to an animal. The deer turned and went into the wood without hurry. It stopped whenever the boy stumbled. It was not a hunt the other way round. It was an invitation. The ground smelled of resin and warm stone.",
    "They came to a spring hidden under a ledge of rock. The water was so clear that the bottom looked near and was deep. The deer drank first, slowly, then moved aside. Nikola filled his waterskin and drank. He washed his face and laughed to himself. The village was half a day down, but the water already had a name: the spring of the golden antlers. He marked the trees with strips of red wool, without hurting them. His grandmother could send the others the next day.",
    "The village came up with jugs, not bows. They built a stone trough, low and smooth, for the sheep and for whoever came later. The deer appeared once, at the end of the afternoon. It drank at the new trough and vanished among the pines. Nobody pointed anything at it. Nikola put his bow away at home, hung high. He began climbing with an empty waterskin instead of an arrow. The Durmitor stayed big. Only the village well stopped answering hollow.",
    "There are mornings when the sun hits the heights and someone swears they see two golden streaks in the trees. It might be light. It might be the deer, which only comes back for people who arrive with open hands. The Montenegrin tale does not turn the hunter into a saint. It turns him into someone who chose the spring instead of the trophy. If you find something rare, first ask what it is for while alive. Sometimes it is for a whole village to drink."
  ],
  "me:2": [
    "Before there was the Bay of Kotor, the coast there was a wall of stone. The sea beat against it without coming in. Three sisters lived up high, and their father had boats that fitted in no shelter. Storms broke the oars against the rock. The eldest sister went down to the foam and spoke to the sea. She spoke as one speaks to an old neighbour: 'Open a harbour. We will look after it.' The sea did not answer with words. It answered with a wave that stopped right before her feet.",
    "The wave left three shells on the sand, one for each sister. The middle one understood the message. A harbour only opens if it belongs to everyone, not to their house. The youngest was afraid of losing what did not exist yet. 'And if other boats fill the water and ours stay outside?' The eldest closed the shell in her hand. 'Then it is not a harbour. It is a cupboard.' All three went up and told their father the price was sharing. He looked at the broken boats and agreed.",
    "At night the sea drew back once, and the stone opened in a blue crack. By morning the crack was a bay. It had quiet water inside and mountains around, like a room without a roof. Their father's boats came in first, because they had asked. At noon a boat from another village came in, and the sisters did not close the mouth of the bay. They tied it beside their own. The orange roofs of Kotor did not exist yet. The ground where they would be born was already dry.",
    "The sisters stayed at the entrance, each with her shell. They showed where the water was deep and where it was a trick. When a storm came from the Adriatic, the bay held all the boats, the family's and the others'. The youngest, who had been afraid, counted the prows and saw that there was room to spare. 'The sea made the room bigger than our table,' she said. Their father put bread on the outside boats. That was the first real harbour: food for those who also took shelter.",
    "That is why Kotor is not just a pretty town among mountains. In the tale, it is a door that three sisters refused to keep for themselves. The bay is still blue because the bargain held. Whoever arrives finds a place, and whoever lives there does not lock the shelter up. If you open the door of your home on a rainy night, do as they did. A harbour that chooses its boats stops being a harbour. The sea, for its part, has already done its bit."
  ],
  "me:3": [
    "Lovćen is the mountain that looks at the sea, above Cetinje, the old capital. From far away, the green back looks like a dragon lying down. Its head is turned to the water, and its tail is lost in the clouds. The shepherds say it is not bad. It is sleeping, and its sleep is what holds the wind. When it dreams restlessly, the olive trees bend and the roof tiles sing. When it dreams quietly, the bay below is smooth as a plate.",
    "There was a month when the dragon moved three times. One night it took a fisherman's hat. Another, it scattered the nets. The third time, the bell of Cetinje rang by itself. The town did not go to war against the mountain. It sent Nikola, who knew an old song on the gusle. The gusle is a one-stringed instrument whose sound is like a voice walking slowly. His grandmother told him: 'Do not wake what sleeps. Keep it company until the dream calms.'",
    "Nikola sat on a stone below the summit, small against the green. He played the song of the olive trees. It was not a song of victory. It said the names of quiet things: the well, the bread, the tied-up boat, the door closed without hurry. The wind, already half up, came down like a dog that knows the voice. The mountain's back stopped trembling. A cloud that looked like a half-open eye closed again. Nikola did not smile with pride. He went on until the string warmed his fingers.",
    "In Cetinje, the roof tiles fell silent. On the coast, the nets dried straight again. The gusle killed no dragon, because there was no dragon to kill. There was a mountain that protects the country when it is at peace with itself. Nikola came down with the instrument on his shoulder. He ate soup without telling of his deeds. He only said: 'It listened.' His grandmother nodded, like someone who already knew the song was old for a reason.",
    "If one day you go to Lovćen, the sea below looks so small it fits in a hand. Remember the tale then. Montenegro put a dragon in its dearest mountain. It asked the dragon to sleep, not to fight. Courage, here, is one string and a song without hurry. Not everything big is beaten. Some big things guard us, as long as someone sits nearby and makes no warlike noise."
  ],
  "me:4": [
    "In a garden in Cetinje there was an apple tree with a single golden apple. Every night the apple disappeared. In the morning it was on the branch again, as if the tree put it back out of stubbornness. The king in the tale is more a master of a big house than a man with heavy crowns. He set guards. The guards fell asleep. The apple left all the same. Then they called Nikola, because he was the one who stayed awake listening to other people's gusle.",
    "Nikola took no net. He took a blanket and stayed under the tree. Near midnight nine peacocks came, white, walking like people who know the garden. The smallest jumped onto the branch and took the apple in its beak. On landing, it shook its feathers. Under the feathers was a girl called Lara, in a gold-embroidered dress, with the apple in both hands. She was not stealing it to hide it. She carried it to the well and washed it.",
    "She put the apple back on the branch before sunrise, so the tree would not dry out. The guards would have shut the door. Nikola said her name quietly, so as not to frighten the other eight. They were sisters and were already turning back into birds. 'You are not taking the apple. You are looking after it. Why at night?' Lara looked at him, surprised not to be seized. 'By day the big house wants the apple on a table, and the tree is left empty. At night I give it back, and the tree goes on.'",
    "Nikola understood. The garden's wealth was the apple coming back, not the apple being eaten once. He went to the master of the house and told him without giving Lara away. He asked that the apple stay on the branch. He asked that the town come and see it in the morning, without picking it. The master, tired of sleeping guards, agreed to a week's trial. The apple stayed there. People passed, touched the skin with one finger and went away.",
    "On the seventh morning Lara appeared without feathers, in the light, with her sisters, and nobody ran. There was bread instead of a trap. She stayed because the garden had learned not to empty the tree. The golden apple of Montenegro is not a prize taken by force. It is a fruit that only shines if it goes back to the branch. Nikola and Lara were married under the apple tree, and the town ate bread and walnuts, not the apple. If you like something beautiful, leave it where it can return."
  ],
  "mk:0": [
    "In Ohrid, Elena helped her grandmother fetch water from a small well. It was so deep that the bucket always came back half empty. The old town went down to the lake, but in that upper neighbourhood water was hard to get. One night the grandmother dreamed that the well was asking for company. In the morning she did not laugh at the dream. She sent Elena down with a jug and with time. Time is the only thing a well accepts from people in a hurry.",
    "Elena put her ear to the stone. She heard a thread of water, not a river. Instead of pulling until the rope broke, she spoke quietly. She spoke as her grandmother spoke to the chickens. 'If you come out, you will not stay only in our bucket. The whole neighbourhood is thirsty.' The water rose a hand's width, then another, and spilled over into the ditch. Elena was frightened and ran to call people. She did not cover the well with a stone.",
    "By midday the ditch was a stream. The stream looked for the slope, like all honest water. It went down between the houses and licked the vegetable gardens. Down below, where the lake is today, it opened out. Nobody became the owner of the spring. They put a low wall around the old well, so the children would not fall in. They put a drinking fountain beside it, for anyone passing. Elena wet her hands and did not keep a drop in her pocket.",
    "The lake grew until the mountains made a bowl for it. The boats of Ohrid learned to move slowly near the reeds. The fish, which nobody had ordered, appeared. The water had grown quiet enough to receive them. Elena went down every day to see whether the well above was still breathing. It was. Her grandmother said that a lake is only a well that agreed to belong to everyone.",
    "That is why, in Ohrid, drinking from the lake is remembering the jug that was not hidden. Elena did not open the earth with a tool. She opened it with a sentence: the water will not stay only in our bucket. If you find a small spring, do not drain it to take it home. Let it run to where more people can kneel down. Big lakes often begin with a small thirst and someone who does not cover the well."
  ],
  "mk:1": [
    "Itar Pejo was the boy the village asked for advice. They asked him when someone wanted to keep all the honey. There was a rich man, owner of many beehives. He measured other people's honey with a small glass and his own with a big one. Pejo was hired to 'sort out the measure'. He did not bring scales. He brought a spoon and a question.",
    "The man showed him the storeroom, the jars and the big glass. Pejo tasted a finger of honey and said it was good. Then he said good honey sours if it does not see the neighbour's light. The man laughed. Pejo suggested a simple rule. Every morning, one spoon from the storeroom would go to the house with no beehive. The big glass would only be used again afterwards. 'If the honey disappears, I owe you,' he said. 'If it gets sweeter, the rule stays.'",
    "A week passed. The houses without beehives put the spoon on the children's bread. Nobody broke into the storeroom, because they no longer needed to. The man was suspicious. He tasted his own honey and found it the same, perhaps clearer. He no longer kept it all day in the dark of a closed jar. Pejo had done no magic. He had set the honey moving, which is the opposite of measuring twice.",
    "At the end of the week the man wanted the big glass back. Pejo returned it, washed. He left the spoon on the table. 'The glass is yours. The spoon belongs to the village.' The man looked at the two things. For the first time, he used the glass to serve someone else, not to serve himself first. The village expected a trick from Pejo. It was left with a rule instead of a joke.",
    "Itar Pejo stayed clever. But the Macedonian tale worth repeating is not the one about trickery. It is the one about the fair measure. Honey does not get sweeter for being hidden. It gets sweeter when a spoon leaves the storeroom and comes back the next day. The house that received it keeps a hive of gratitude. If you have too much, the cleverest thing is the spoon, not the big glass."
  ],
  "mk:2": [
    "On the Pelister, the mountain of pines, Elena climbed to pick a rare flower. The Bitola fair paid well for it. The flower was small and pink. It grew in a place where the grass made a circle. She already had her hand out when she heard music without an instrument. She stopped. The samovilas are the mountain fairies. They do not like people picking what marks their home.",
    "They did not appear in anger. They appeared tall, their hair wet with cloud, dancing slowly at the edge of the circle. One of them, the youngest, pointed at the flower. Then she pointed at Elena's empty basket. 'That one stays. There is heather further down, and it is enough for the fair.' Elena pulled back her hand. She had climbed so far that it hurt to go down with nothing. It hurt more to think of carrying a door away in her basket.",
    "The young samovila went with her to the heather. She showed her how to cut the long stem without pulling up the root. 'You take the branch. The plant stays for next year.' Elena filled the basket with what would grow again. In the circle, the rare flower stayed in its place. The music died down until it sounded like wind. Nobody asked her for payment. They asked her not to come back with a spade.",
    "In Bitola, the heather sold well enough for the week's bread. Elena told her grandmother about the circle and the flower that did not come. Her grandmother knew the mountain from before the maps. She nodded. 'The samovilas do not hide beauty. They hide hurry.' The next Sunday Elena climbed only as far as the heather. She left a little bread on a stone outside the circle. She went down before she felt like being cleverer than agreed.",
    "The Pelister is still full of pines and of places where the grass lies in a round. The tale asks for one thing only. The rare flower stays where the dance put it. What can be taken is what grows again. If you walk into a circle by accident, walk out without taking a sample. The fairies of Macedonia do not charge for a fright. They charge that the mountain is the same when the music ends."
  ],
  "mk:3": [
    "Krali Marko had a horse called Šarac, splashed with white and dark. It was so strong that the village asked it to open a path in the snow. Marko liked to set off early and to arrive first. That day Šarac would not leave the stable. It was not ill. It had its ear turned to the road, where a broken cart was holding up a family. The cart carried sacks of wool.",
    "Marko pulled the rein. The horse planted its hooves. The family was not asking for a hero. They were asking someone to lift the axle without breaking the wheel. Marko was already late for a feast in Skopje. He grumbled and dismounted. Only then did Šarac take a step, and it went towards the cart, not forward. The splashed horse had chosen the work before the road.",
    "They lifted the axle with a beam. The wheel fitted. The wool did not go into the mud. The family's mother offered bread. Marko was used to refusing what seemed small. He accepted because Šarac was already eating the oats held out to it. 'You do not move if I do not stop,' he said to the horse, half angry and half laughing. Šarac shook his mane, like someone confirming an old contract.",
    "They reached Skopje after the feast had begun, and nobody was any poorer for it. The cart, though, arrived in one piece. Marko understood that the horse's strength was not a shortcut. It was a condition. Šarac carried a storybook king only when the king dismounted in the right place. The village began to say: if the splashed one will not move, look behind you, not at the spur.",
    "The tale of Krali Marko has versions with fighting. This one stays with the version of the road. The horse Šarac is not a machine. It is the one that refuses to go on when there is a broken wheel behind. Courage, here, is dismounting. If your step stalls for no clear reason, look behind you. Sometimes strength is waiting for an axle, for bread and for a feast you can reach later."
  ],
  "mk:4": [
    "The Kale fortress in Skopje sits on the town like an open hand of stone. Elena climbed the steps with a small secret. She had promised a friend to look after a bead necklace during the fair. The fair had ended without her friend appearing. The necklace weighed in her pocket. Giving it back the next day was easy. Spending it on apples was the idea the stone seemed to hear.",
    "She sat on a wide wall, where generations had sat down with promises. There was no ghost's voice. There was the wind of the Vardar and the view of the roofs. Elena took out the necklace and saw beads just like the ones her friend's grandmother wore. She understood that an apple lasts a moment and a word lasts as long as the wall. She closed the necklace in her pocket. She went down before the pretty view changed her mind.",
    "Her friend arrived at nightfall, late because of a sheep that had got loose. Elena handed over the necklace without making a speech. Her friend held it tight and asked whether it had been hard. 'It was the wall,' said Elena. 'From up there, apples look more important.' They laughed, and went to eat an apple bought with other money. That money was really their own.",
    "The Kale did not keep the necklace. It kept the interval in which she could have spent it and did not. That is what the stone knows how to do: stay while people decide. Elena went up there again on Sunday, with no full pockets, just to see the river. The town below made its noise. The promise no longer weighed. The wall was still wide enough for someone else to sit down.",
    "In Skopje, the tale of the Kale stone is not about hidden treasure. It is about something given back. Fortresses look as if they are made for war, and many were. This one, for children, stays a high place. From there you see more clearly what belongs to others. If you sit on a wall with what is not yours, come down with it still in your pocket. The view is free. The necklace is not."
  ],
  "mn:0": [
    "The round felt house is called a ger. Its door faced south, so the sun could come into the plain without asking. Temuulen arrived for a visit near Ulan Bator. He wore a long blue coat and an orange sash, and carried his fur hat in his hand. Inside the hat rests. His grandfather stopped him before the wooden door. 'Do not step on the threshold,' he said. 'Step over it, as if the wood were a narrow river.'",
    "Inside, the left side was the way of the guests. Temuulen was nervous and happy. He was about to sit in the place of the host, at the back, where you see the door. His grandfather did not make a speech to shame him. He tapped the mat lightly, on the right side. Temuulen moved, with warm ears and his smile still whole. 'Here,' said his grandfather. 'The back is kept for whoever opens the house.'",
    "They brought hot tea with milk in small bowls. Temuulen received his with both hands and drank slowly. He did not put the bowl down carelessly on the ground. Saying thank you was part of the taste. The woman of the house nodded, pleased, and nobody spoke of the threshold again. The mistake had been short, and the correction was short too. Outside, the wind went round the ger. It did not come in through the south door uninvited.",
    "Before leaving, Temuulen stepped over the threshold again without touching it. He thanked his hosts facing the door. His grandfather was beside him, pleased without fuss. On the way, the boy repeated the three rules quietly. Do not step on the wood. Remember the door faces south. Walk first towards the left. It was not a lesson to make visits smaller. It was the way for a visit to fit in the house without upsetting it.",
    "If you go into a round felt house, do not step on the threshold. Do not sit in the place of your host either. Temuulen, near Ulan Bator, changed mats when his grandfather tapped lightly. He drank the tea and said thank you. You can get a step wrong and put it right without staying small for ever. The south door is still open to anyone who arrives with care. Tea tastes better in the right place."
  ],
  "mn:1": [
    "On the road to Karakorum there was a pile of stones made by many hands. Some were big at the bottom and little ones sat on top. Faded ribbons moved in the wind. Temuulen's grandfather stopped. 'Add one,' he said. 'Take none away.' The boy chose a smooth pebble. He put it on top with care, so no stone would fall. They walked round the pile once, the way his grandfather did. Then they went on.",
    "Further on, a visitor was putting a stone from the pile into his pocket. Maybe he wanted a keepsake. Maybe he was not thinking. It was not a mistake made out of wickedness. It was still a mistake. Temuulen did not run to shout at him. He did not pull his sleeve. He spoke clearly, at the height of his own voice. 'That one stays on the pile,' he said. 'The keepsake is the walk you did, not the stone you carry.'",
    "The visitor went red and laughed at himself, with no argument. He put the stone back, a little crooked. Temuulen helped him steady it with two smaller ones, so it would not fall into someone's path. The visitor said thank you and went on lighter. His pocket was empty and the story was in its place. Nobody needed to push anybody. The pile kept the same height as before, just a little straighter on top.",
    "His grandfather had seen from far off. He did not turn the moment into a long lesson in front of strangers. He only said, already walking: 'You spoke without raising your voice. That holds the pile too.' Temuulen kept the pebble that was his, picked up from the path, so as not to confuse the two things. At home, his mother put a small river stone on the shelf. Some stones are carried, and some are left.",
    "If you pass a pile of stones on the road to Karakorum, leave a small stone. Walk round it, and do not keep in your pocket what many people put there. Temuulen knew how to say the right sentence without arguing. You too can put a mistake right politely, and the stone goes back to the pile whole. A keepsake fits in the head. It does not need to fit in anyone's pocket."
  ],
  "mn:2": [
    "In Karakorum, his grandfather had a wooden instrument with two strings. A horse's head was carved at the end, its ears alert, its mane still in the carving. Temuulen wanted to pull the strings hard, so the sound would come out big as the plain. A dry sound came out, with no song in it. His grandfather put a hand over his. 'This horse does not hurry,' he said. 'You hear the step, not the pull.'",
    "The grandfather told the story used in this house. The horse was already old and stayed in the pasture with the right step. The instrument had been carved to remember that walk. In the song the horse goes on living, crossing the plain whenever the strings sound slowly. There was no hurry and no farewell. There was good memory. Temuulen looked at the carved head and lowered his fingers, waiting for the bow.",
    "They practised the light bow, almost tickling the string. The sound grew long, like a trot heard from far away. Real horses pass behind the hill and leave only the rhythm. Temuulen smiled. His grandfather let him repeat until his arm understood by itself. Too much force spoils the step. Then they put the instrument away standing up, not lying on the ground. The horse's head looked at the door of the ger.",
    "At night, his grandfather played the whole song. Temuulen only kept time with his foot on the ground, marking the step. Outside, a real horse snorted in the dark, awake and well. It seemed to answer without knowing the words. The boy did not go and pull its mane to confirm anything. He stayed listening. The song and the animal were relatives, not the same thing. Both were fine where they were, one in the wood and the other in the pasture.",
    "If you play an instrument with a horse's head, do not pull the strings like someone hurrying an animal. Temuulen learned the light bow in Karakorum. The horse of the song stayed alive in the sound. It had the step of one that grows old in the pasture. It never stops walking. You can remember a step with music, without turning it into a race. The right walk is heard better when the hand goes low."
  ],
  "mn:3": [
    "In the Gobi desert, the ground looks dry until a two-humped camel stops. It sniffs at a darker patch. Temuulen was going with his uncle and two neighbours. He wore a fur hat and a well-tied orange sash. The camel was not a mount for hurrying. It was the companion that knew thirst. They stopped well away from its feet. 'You do not dig under the one that has already found water,' said his uncle. 'You dig beside it.'",
    "The adults opened the damp earth with short spades, beside the camel. The camel lay down calmly, without being pushed. Temuulen took out small stones with his hands, always out of reach of the feet. The water did not jump up like a party. It came slowly, darkening the bottom of a shallow hole. Soon there was enough to fill a flask. The boy held the flask with both hands. He was proud of the little, which was the right size for that day's thirst.",
    "They each drank a mouthful. The camel had its share in a shallow tin, without being made to drink from the open hole. Then they covered the hole with a clean board. On top they put a marker of stones, clearly visible. That way the next people would find the water, and the animals would not put a foot in it. His uncle made a mark on the board, pointing towards the path. The place was not to look like a closed secret.",
    "On the way back, Temuulen looked behind him once. The marker showed against the pale ground. The camel walked on, two humps against the sky, in no hurry to be a hero of the story. The boy told the tale at home. He did not say he had dug alone, because he had not. He said the names of those who held the spade. He spoke of the camel with respect. His mother gave him fresh water and told him to tell a good thirst truthfully too.",
    "If a camel finds a damp patch, do not dig under its feet. Do not leave the hole open either. Temuulen waited and helped beside the hole. The adults covered the water with a board and a marker of stones. You can be thirsty and still leave the place safe. That is for whoever comes next, person or animal. One full flask, shared, is enough for the day. The camel goes on, and the shallow well stays covered."
  ],
  "mn:4": [
    "By Lake Khövsgöl, far from the lights of Ulan Bator, seven old stars appear early. They look like a quiet cart in the sky. Temuulen lay on the dry grass, near the ger, with his blue coat closed. He wanted to stay all night counting the rest of the sky. The stars seemed close enough for a promise. His grandfather sat beside him and did not laugh at the wish. 'They are big, and a bed is a serious place too,' he said.",
    "His grandfather suggested a household measure. They would stay the time of two stars, long enough to see the cart move a tiny bit. Then they would go in. Temuulen thought the measure was small and accepted anyway, because it was not a punishment. He pointed out the seven, one by one. He gave them the names of uncles who were not there, just for fun. His grandfather corrected one name, calmly. The other six stayed in play until the measure ended.",
    "The two stars of the measure did their bit of travelling. By then the cold was already living in the sleeves of the blue coat. The ger had a low light in the door, facing south, as Temuulen had learned. His grandfather got up first and held out his hand, not to pull him, but to invite him. The boy looked at the seven once more. They were not going away just because he went to sleep. They stayed over the lake, on duty.",
    "Inside, the tea with milk was still warm. Temuulen took off his fur hat and sat on his side, without stepping on the threshold. He was so used to it that he was already smiling at the care. Through the opening at the top of the ger a piece of sky could be seen. In that piece there was room for one star, not all seven, but it was proof enough. 'Tomorrow they are there,' said his grandfather. The old stars do not miss an agreement.",
    "If you want to stay all night under an enormous sky, do as Temuulen did by Lake Khövsgöl. Love the stars and still go in when the measure ends. You can like something very big and go to bed all the same. The seven old stars go on looking at the lake and the plain. The ger keeps you safe while they stay outside, quiet, doing their usual work."
  ],
  "ni:0": [
    "Marisol wore an embroidered blouse, a red skirt and a flower crown. She climbed onto a boat going to Ometepe, the island of the two volcanoes. The two mountains were calm, without noise and without hurry. They looked like two people sitting to look at the water. The boatman followed the known route, through the middle of the great lake. She went with her grandfather and a notebook on her lap. Nobody spoke of reaching the summit that day.",
    "On the island, they began on a marked path, with small stones at the sides and a wooden arrow. Her grandfather said you only leave the trail when you know the bush. They did not know it. Marisol walked in the middle of the path, without shortcuts. The two mountains looked better from there, still quiet. A bird flew across and did not frighten them.",
    "Beside the path there was a wide stone with a spiral drawing. It was worn by time and still clear. Marisol knelt to see it, without putting her foot on it. She moved her finger in the air and copied the curve, not on the stone. Her grandfather explained that it was an old sign. It was meant to be looked at and not changed. The spiral looked like a snail of lines, patient.",
    "Marisol opened her notebook and drew the spiral on the paper. She drew slowly, looking and looking again. She had a piece of chalk in her pocket. She thought of marking the stone so she would remember. Then she put the chalk away without using it. 'Paper is enough,' said her grandfather. The stone stayed as it was. The new drawing travelled in the notebook, not on the island.",
    "They went back along the same marked path. They left no mark and no paper on the ground. The two volcanoes stayed calm behind them, and the boat waited on the shore. Marisol showed the drawn spiral. She was pleased with what she had not spoilt. If you see an old drawing on a stone, copy it on paper. You do not draw lines on the stone. You stay on the path that is already made."
  ],
  "ni:1": [
    "In Granada, beside Lake Nicaragua, the fishermen spoke of a very big fish. It lives at the bottom and almost never shows itself. This was not frightened talk. It was respectful talk, said while they mended nets in the shade. Marisol listened, sitting on an upturned bucket. Her uncle nodded when the fish's name came up. Nobody was going to look for it that day.",
    "One fisherman said the big fish has the lake as its deep home. Their nets are not for that home. They let it pass, if they ever feel it down there. Then they go on with the small work. Marisol asked why, with her eyes wide open. 'Because there are neighbours you do not visit,' he answered, laughing without mockery. The lake lay smooth, without any fuss.",
    "Even so, Marisol wanted to go out in a boat to the middle. She wanted just to peep, and to say she had seen it. She imagined only a slow shadow at the bottom, far from her feet. She remembered that imagining is not calling. Her uncle shook his head calmly. 'Wanting to see does not oblige a neighbour to appear,' he said. Marisol agreed to stay where the nets were small.",
    "They went out in a shallow boat, near the shore. They caught only what fitted in the lunch pot. Her uncle worked slowly, without noise, and Marisol helped wet the rope. A fish came up so small it still looked made of clear glass. Her uncle let it go at once with a wet hand. It was back in the water in a moment. 'That one is not yet,' he said.",
    "Lunch was small fish cooked in the pot. The big fish at the bottom was not even named at the table. Marisol understood that a lake can have secrets and still be a calm place. There was no chase and no running boat. If you hear about a neighbour like that, leave them in their home. You catch only what arrives, and you give back what is still too small."
  ],
  "ni:2": [
    "In the market in León there was music and people laughing between the fruit stalls. A gentleman explained the Güegüense to Marisol. It is an old dance in which you answer vanity with a step and a smile. You do not answer with a fight. The dancers used wide gestures, and the crowd clapped without pushing. Marisol stood watching, with the empty basket in her arms. It looked like a game of gentle answers.",
    "An adult at the stall, in a tall hat, saw her so attentive. He asked a vain question, joking. 'Do you think you can dance better than me, girl with the flower crown?' It was not an insult, but it was a vain challenge. Some people turned, waiting. Marisol felt her ears grow hot and did not lower her eyes. She took a deep breath, as her grandmother had taught her.",
    "She did not call him names, or take his hat, or close her hand. She did not run away between the stalls either. She looked at the smooth floor of the market and remembered the dancers' feet. The music was still in the air, light. Marisol decided that the answer could be a movement, not a hard word. She turned once more to look at the dancers and thought of their steps.",
    "She took a small, clear step to the side. Then she made a turn with her hands open, smiling. The adult in the hat was surprised. Instead of pressing on, he answered with another step, laughing too. There was no pushing and no angry raised voice. The market laughed kindly, as if the square had understood the joke. Marisol finished with a little bow.",
    "The gentleman with the hat took it off for a moment, in greeting. He offered her an orange without asking anything in return. Marisol accepted and went back to her grandmother, still smiling. The dance had answered, and nobody went away hurt. If someone says something vain to you, you can answer with grace. You do not need to hurt anyone or shout to make yourself heard."
  ],
  "ni:3": [
    "Near Granada, the boat went among the islets. These are tiny islands with trees, houses and gardens at the water's edge. Marisol went in front, counting islands until she lost the count. Some had jetties, others only birds and branches. Her uncle slowed down so the wave would not hit the banks. The trip was for looking, not for invading.",
    "They passed an island with a very tidy garden. There were red flowers beside the water and a quiet house behind. There was no stall and no invitation, only a private path and an empty chair. Nobody had said that visitors were welcome there. Marisol saw a flower so close she could almost touch it. The boat slowed even more. She leaned over, to smell without picking.",
    "Marisol's fingers stopped in the air. She remembered that the garden had an owner, even if the owner was not at the window. 'It is not ours,' she said quietly, and sat up straight. Her uncle nodded, pleased, without making a speech. The flower stayed on the branch, moving in the lake breeze. Her fingers stayed empty, and the garden stayed as it was.",
    "From a small balcony, someone waved. Marisol waved back, with an open hand and without asking for anything. She did not jump onto the island or ask her uncle to pull in. At the bottom of the boat there was a ripe mango, brought from home. Her uncle peeled it calmly and split the slices. They both ate in their own boat, watching the islands go by.",
    "When they got back to Granada, Marisol had sticky hands from the mango. There was no hidden flower in her basket. She told her grandmother she had waved at a garden that was not hers. Her grandmother gave her water to wash her fingers and praised the wave. If you pass a garden that belongs to someone else, leave the flowers where they are. You can wave and share what you brought in your own boat."
  ],
  "ni:4": [
    "At home, her grandmother was making pinol. It is a drink of toasted maize, stirred with water until it is smooth and cool. The smell was of an old kitchen, simple and sweet. Marisol climbed onto a stool to reach the big bowl. Her grandfather was already sitting, waiting, with the empty cup in front of him. Her cousin watched with folded arms, smiling like someone who had not yet tasted it.",
    "Her grandmother passed the spoon to Marisol. 'Stir all the time, or it goes lumpy,' she said. Marisol stirred slowly, scraping the bottom. She stirred until the pinol was the same all through the bowl, without lumps of flour. Her cousin said it was too much work for an old drink. Marisol did not stop to argue, even when her arm grew a little tired.",
    "When it was smooth, her grandmother held the bowl and let Marisol serve. The first cup went to her grandfather, who thanked her with a nod. Only then did Marisol fill her own, a smaller one, and take a sip. She did not serve herself first, even though she was thirsty. Her grandfather said it was right. The kitchen was happy without a big party.",
    "Her cousin laughed and said that was a drink for old people. It was not for girls at the fair. Marisol held out the spoon with a little still on it, without getting cross. Her cousin tasted it, pulled a short face and then went quiet. She asked for a cup, and then asked for more. The old laugh fell silent, and nobody made fun of her for changing her mind.",
    "The three of them drank their cups in the yard. The cousin helped wash the bowl without being told twice. The pinol was finished, and the wish to make it another day stayed. Marisol put the spoon back in her grandmother's place. If you taste an old drink, stir it with care. Serve first whoever is in front of you. You can laugh after tasting, not before you know the taste."
  ]
};

